Indeed, if $L\phi=k^2\phi$, <differentiation> and this identity show
$$
(L-k^2)(A\phi+\partial_t\phi)
=([L,A]-L_t)\phi=0.
$$
Thus $\partial_t+A$ preserves every spectral eigenspace, which is the <isospectral Lax equation> formulation.

